  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 705 PM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Severe Thunderstorm Watch issued for most of \ncentral NC through 100 AM. Main threat damaging wind. Secondary \nthreat isolated tornado.\n\nThere continues to be an isolated tornado threat within a\nlarger area of damaging thunderstorm wind threat as new storms\ndevelop into north-central NC. There continues to be a low level\nboundary (lee trough/outflow) in a SSW to NNE fashion from the\nTriad into VA. Low level wind shear 30-35 knots exists with the\nboundary. Severe storms have been rotating given the shear and\nboundary. Mix layer CAPE is maximized 2000 j/kg over the central\nPiedmont. \n\nDeep convection will probably continue to develop into central\nNC this evening, Shear profiles will be supportive of \nsupercells, particularly where coverage remains scattered and \ncells discrete, versus a tendency for bowing segments within the\nbroken line of frontal convection. Storms should be enhanced\nthrough late evening, and likely weakening as they move SE into\nthe Coastal Plain later. \n\nAgain, a tornado is possible with any of the initial\nsupercells. Incidents of wind damage are likely in the\ndeveloping clusters/bow segments. \n\nKey Message 2: Dry through Saturday, then unsettled into next \nweek. \n\nLate Week through Weekend: High pressure shifts from the Upper \nMississippi Valley on Wednesday, settling over the Mid-Atlantic by \nFriday. Expect near to below-average temperatures and fair weather \nthrough Saturday afternoon. The next round of precipitation arrives \nlate Saturday evening into Sunday morning as tropical moisture \ninteracts with a developing stationary boundary.\n\nInto Next Week: Unsettled weather persists through at least mid-week \nas the stationary boundary lingers overhead. Plentiful Gulf and \nAtlantic moisture will tap into this lingering  boundary, bringing \nmultiple rounds of showers and isolated thunderstorms across the \nregion over several days.",
